2. Substitute $ \rho_t $ from the PDE and split into two integrals.  
3. Evaluate integrals via integration by parts, assuming $ \rho(t, 0) = 0 $ (positive speeds only) and neglecting boundary terms at infinity, yielding $ \frac{dN}{dt} = u(t) - N(t) $.  
4. Substitute $ u(t) = u_0 + u_1 N(t) $ to obtain $ \frac{dN}{dt} = u_0 + (u_1 - 1)N(t) $.  
5. Solve the ODE: for $ u_1 > 1 $, $ N(t) $ grows exponentially, proving unboundedness.  

Part (2):  
