2. Solve \( 2^n + 5^n - n \equiv 0 \pmod{125} \): For \( n \geq 3 \), \( 5^n \equiv 0 \pmod{125} \), reducing to \( 2^n \equiv n \pmod{125} \). Use Euler’s theorem (\( 2^{100} \equiv 1 \pmod{125} \)) to express \( 2^n \equiv 2^{n \mod 100} \pmod{125} \), then solve \( 2^s \equiv 100m + s \pmod{125} \) where \( n = 100m + s \).
3. Combine results: Find the smallest \( n \equiv 5 \pmod{8} \) that satisfies \( 2^n \equiv n \pmod{125} \). Use modular arithmetic to align the periodicity of \( 2^n \) with the linear term \( n \), leveraging the order of 6 modulo 125 (since \( 2^8 \equiv 6 \pmod{125} \)) to solve \( 32 \cdot 6^k \equiv 8k + 5 \pmod{125} \).
4. Verify the solution modulo 1000 by checking \( 2^n \mod 1000 \), \( 5^n \mod 1000 \), and \( n \mod 1000 \).

